    brief: `This page is the heart of the Trimpe part. Source: Fiedler, Menn, Kreisköther, Trimpe, "On Safety in Safe Bayesian Optimization", TMLR 2024 (arXiv 2403.12948) — READ THE FULL TEXT (local copy) and reproduce its logic faithfully.
1. The gap: (a) the "β problem": implementations use β ≡ 2 (Berkenkamp 2016; Turchetta 2016), 3 (Helwa 2019; GoSafe), 4 (GoSafeOpt) instead of the theoretical β_t, so the frequentist guarantee is void; (b) the "RKHS norm problem": the bound B on ||f||_k cannot be obtained from engineering knowledge and the true norm is easily off by an order of magnitude; (c) Gaussian noise vs bounded noise assumptions. Quote Table 1 exactly: 100 SE-RKHS functions with norm 10, 10^4 runs each: SafeOpt β≡2: unsafe runs 3.95% on average, 28.62% worst function; Real-β-SafeOpt with under-estimated B = 2.5: 0.859% (13.38% worst); Real-β-SafeOpt with the true norm: 0 unsafe but never left the initial points in 30.40% of runs; LoSBO: 0% unsafe and 90.90% final performance (vs 82.45%). Also: with β≡2, 2727 ± 3882 of 10^4 datasets per function violated the confidence tube.
2. Real-β-SafeOpt: use the Abbasi-Yadkori-form β (eq. 7 of the paper: β_t = B + (R/√λ) sqrt(2 ln(δ^{-1} det(I + λ^{-1} K_t)^{1/2}))) — show it coincides with the corrected AAAI-2021 bound for 0 < λ < 1 (module 3); discuss the conservatism trade-off (with the true norm it is safe but may not explore) and why under-estimating B silently breaks it.
3. LoSBO: assumptions (f L-Lipschitz w.r.t. metric d; noise |ε_t| ≤ E; f ≥ h on S_0); safe-set update S_t = S_{t−1} ∪ {x ∈ D : y_{t−1} − E − L d(x_{t−1}, x) ≥ h} (eq. 11); Proposition 1 (all queries safe for ANY β_t > 0) with its one-line proof; the GP is used only for the acquisition (expanders/maximisers from the GP band), so exploration quality depends on β but safety does not; Remark 2 (asymmetric/time-varying noise bounds, general moduli of continuity — Hölder) and Remark 3 (E = 2 B_ε in experiments); the honest statement that SafeOpt's convergence proof does not carry over (the paper notes a step fails) and under-exploration is not ruled out.
4. LoS-GP-UCB: gridless version: safe set as a union of balls B̄_{r_j}(z_j) with r_j from the Lipschitz/noise argument; acquisition maximises μ + β σ over the union (eq. 15–16); scaling to moderate dimensions.
5. MCLoSBO (Menn, Pelizzari, Fleps-Dezasse, Trimpe CDC 2024): multiple safety constraints, automotive control tuning in simulation and on a test vehicle; how multi-constraint safe sets intersect.
6. Event-triggered / time-varying: Brunzema et al. ET-GP-UCB (TMLR 2025): a GP-error-bound trigger detects change and resets data — give the trigger condition; ETSO (Holzapfel, Brunzema, Trimpe L4DC 2024): event-triggered SafeOpt on quadcopters — what is reset and the caveat (heuristically scaled threshold in experiments); Brunzema CDC 2022 (time-varying BO for controller tuning).
7. Consequences: "safety should rest only on assumptions a user can check" (Lipschitz constants and noise bounds are engineering quantities; RKHS norms are not); the companion short paper (arXiv 2501.13697) — cite the TMLR paper; open problems: RKHS-norm estimation (Tokmak et al. AISTATS 2025; PACSBO; Wenzel–Tokmak–Fiedler 2026 superconvergence), hyperparameter adaptation, Gaussian noise violating hard bounds, exploration guarantees for Lipschitz-only methods.
Walkthrough (5 steps): the LoSBO safety proof by induction: base case S_0 safe; induction step: if x_{t−1} ∈ S_{t−1} is safe and y_{t−1} = f(x_{t−1}) + ε_{t−1} with |ε| ≤ E, then for any x with y_{t−1} − E − L d(x_{t−1}, x) ≥ h we have f(x) ≥ f(x_{t−1}) − L d ≥ y_{t−1} − E − L d ≥ h. Explain each inequality.
Explorer "Break SafeOpt, not LoSBO": Monte-Carlo in the browser: sample K random SE-RKHS functions with a chosen norm (slider 2–20) on a 40-point grid, run both SafeOpt (β slider, default 2, as in practice) and LoSBO (E and L sliders; L computed as the true max slope of the sampled function times a user "L-estimate factor" slider to show what happens when L is under-estimated) for 25 iterations each, count unsafe evaluations; show the fraction of runs with ≥ 1 violation for both, plus the average final regret. Seeded PRNG; "resample" button. Use real GP math (reuse the small solver). Show that LoSBO has zero violations when L is not under-estimated, SafeOpt violates for small β.
Exercises: prove Proposition 1; show that Real-β with B < ||f||_k can be unsafe by constructing a function; convert a Hölder modulus into a LoSBO safe-set rule; compute the LoSBO safe set for numerical data; explain why Gaussian noise breaks the LoSBO assumption and how E = 2B_ε helps in practice.
Local sources: losbo (2403.12948), 2105.02796, 2501.12969, 2312.08058, 2208.10790, 2207.11120, 2503.10352, 2501.13697 (if present).`,
