    brief: `Sources (local): GoSafe 2105.13281 (use the arXiv version — it corrects the ICRA'21 safe-set update: ∪ S_{n−1} added, and the rule l_n = max{L_x μ, l_n} for S3 points was removed), GoSafeOpt 2201.09562 (AIJ 2023), SafeMDP 1606.04753, SNO-MDP 2008.06626, ActSafe 2410.09486, Safe exploration via policy priors 2601.19612, Turchetta 2019 (interactive ML) 1910.13726, Prajapat SageMPC 2402.06562.
1. Why local fails: SafeOpt only reaches the ε-reachable set R̄_ε(S_0); a safe region disconnected in parameter space is invisible. Illustrate with a 2-D parameter space sketch.
2. Dynamical setting: policy parameters a, closed-loop trajectory ξ(t, x_0, a), constraint g_i(a) = min over the trajectory of ḡ_i(x) (Assumption 2.5 of GoSafeOpt) — safety is a trajectory property; the state is measured every Δt and moves at most Ξ between samples (Assumption 2.4). Explain why this makes "backups" possible.
3. GoSafe (Baumann, Marco, Turchetta, Trimpe ICRA 2021): explore in the augmented space (a, x_0); three stages S1 (local SafeOpt in the augmented space), S2 (learn boundary conditions: from which states is a backup guaranteed), S3 (global exploration of unsafe-looking parameters with the backup triggered by the boundary condition); why it does not scale (the augmented space is high-dimensional).
4. GoSafeOpt (Sukhija, Turchetta, Lindner, Krause, Trimpe, Baumann AIJ 2023): exploration in parameter space only; the Markov property implies every state visited during a safe rollout is a valid backup point (Prop. A.3); the set of backup pairs B_n; the online boundary condition — trigger the backup at state x iff no (a_s, x_s) ∈ B_n satisfies l_n(a_s, i) ≥ L_x(||x − x_s|| + Ξ) for all i (eq. 12) — and the choice of a_s^* (argmax of the tightest margin); alternation of LSE and GE phases; pseudocode.
5. Guarantees: state Theorem 4.1 (all experiments safe w.p. ≥ 1−δ under Assumptions 2.1–2.5: known safe seed; RKHS-norm bounded constraints; sub-Gaussian noise; state measured every Δt with motion bound Ξ; trajectory-min constraints) and Theorem 4.3 (convergence to the safe global optimum under its extra conditions); explain the Ξ-tolerance lemma: g_i(a_s, x(t)) ≥ g_i(a_s, x(kΔt)) − L_x Ξ ≥ 0 for the state between samples; note that a faster, more conservative boundary condition keeps safety but loses the optimality guarantee.
6. Experiments and the β caveat: Franka Emika Panda (20 simulated runs, 3 hardware runs, 100% safe; pendulum simulation 99.9% average, failures in the local phase); GoSafe used β ≡ 3 and GoSafeOpt β ≡ 4 (documented in the TMLR 2024 paper) — so the hardware runs were outside the formal guarantee; connect to module 5 (a warning-box).
7. Safe exploration in MDPs: SafeMDP (Turchetta, Berkenkamp, Krause NeurIPS 2016): safety function over states, ergodicity via reachability R(S) and returnability R^ret(S, S̄) = S̄ ∪ {s ∈ S : ∃a, f(s,a) ∈ S̄} (note the correct argument order), the expansion operator; SNO-MDP (Wachi & Sui ICML 2020: safe near-optimal in CMDPs with GP models of safety and reward); Turchetta et al. 2019 (goal-oriented safe exploration); ActSafe (As, Sukhija et al. ICLR 2025: model-based active exploration with high-probability safety during learning; optimism–pessimism; sample complexity) and SOOPER / policy priors (ICLR 2026); Prajapat et al. SageMPC (TAC 2025: safe guaranteed exploration for nonlinear systems with MPC). Compare in a table: what is modelled (function of parameters / of states), what is assumed, what is guaranteed.
Walkthrough (5 steps): the backup argument: (1) a safe rollout visits states x(kΔt); (2) Markov property ⇒ from any visited state the same policy a_s is safe for the rest (Prop. A.3); (3) between samples the state moved at most Ξ; (4) Lipschitz in x ⇒ constraint margin drops by at most L_x Ξ; (5) hence the boundary condition l_n(a_s,i) ≥ L_x(||x − x_s|| + Ξ) certifies switching to a_s at x. Insight per step.
Explorer: 2-D parameter space (a_1, a_2) ∈ [0,1]² with a known-to-the-explorer smooth objective whose safe set (g ≥ 0) consists of two disconnected islands (island A contains the safe seed; the global optimum is in island B); mode selector "SafeOpt (local)" vs "GoSafeOpt (global with backups)"; the explorer runs a simplified but faithful loop: local phase = SafeOpt-style expansion with a GP on a 25×25 grid; global phase = jump to an unexplored parameter, simulate a short rollout (a toy 1-D dynamical system whose trajectory-minimum constraint depends on (a, x)), trigger the backup when the boundary condition fails, and add the new region if the rollout stays safe. Display: heatmap of the true g, safe set, evaluated points, backups used, violations (should be 0 in global mode with backups; show what happens if the user disables the boundary condition — violations appear). Seeded PRNG, step/run buttons.
Exercises: prove Prop. A.3 from the Markov property; derive the Ξ-tolerance inequality; compute the returnability operator for a small MDP; show why exploring in the augmented space scales badly (dimension count); design a boundary condition for a given L_x and Ξ.`,
